quick_facts: Shielded rooms for highly radioactive work
quick_facts: Master-slave manipulator arms reach through walls
quick_facts: Lead-glass viewing windows up to ~1 m thick
quick_facts: Used for spent-fuel research, isotope processing, reprocessing R&D
quick_facts: Air pressure kept negative so nothing leaks outward

sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"Nuclear Fuel Cycle Facilities","url":"https://www.iaea.org/"}

definition:
    A heavily shielded room for hands-off handling of intensely radioactive materials. Workers stay outside lead, steel, or thick concrete walls and manipulate equipment through master-slave mechanical arms while watching through lead-glass windows — dense panes up to a meter thick that still let light through. Hot cells are where spent fuel is examined, medical isotopes are processed, and vitrified waste is packed; nothing inside needs a human body anywhere near it.
